saveOutputs: false
cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "# 11b · Step 2 – What happened on the trail? (Spatial streaming)\n\nJonas' watch records every five seconds where he is and how he moves. Shortly after eleven o'clock its curves change. The stream tells us what happened before Jonas tells anyone – if we read it correctly, even with points that arrive late from a dead zone.\n\n**Question:** What happened on the way, when and where exactly?\n\n- **Features:** window functions over event time, `MATCH_RECOGNIZE`, `SDO_ANYINTERACT`, `SDO_NN`, `SDO_GEOM.SDO_DISTANCE`\n- **Pattern:** M4 – detect an event, trigger a query\n- **Data:** `WATCH_STREAM` (Jonas' watch, one point every 5 seconds), `ZONES` (dead zone), `TRAIL_SEGMENTS` (loop trail on OSM geometry)\n- **Run:** as the demo user (DOAG2026) in SQL Developer for VS Code, after `01_run_all.sql`. Run the cells from top to bottom.\n\nThe tables hold the complete recording of 16 November 2026. All queries order by **event time** (`EVENT_TS`, when a point was measured), never by arrival time (`INGEST_TS`).\n\n> Fictitious demo: no medical device and no medical advice. Places from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL); all persons and story places are synthetic."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Session settings\n\nThe story takes place on Monday, 16 November 2026, in Franconia. With the session time zone `Europe/Berlin`, all timestamps and literals read in local time.\n\nThe SQLcl settings in the second cell make the text output readable: columns as wide as their content instead of their declared length (`SQLFORMAT ansiconsole`; values from JSON are `VARCHAR2(4000)`), one header per result (`PAGESIZE`), wider lines (`LINESIZE`) and complete JSON documents and CLOBs (`LONG`, `LONGCHUNKSIZE`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "ALTER SESSION SET TIME_ZONE = 'Europe/Berlin';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SET SQLFORMAT ansiconsole\nSET PAGESIZE 1000\nSET LINESIZE 250\nSET LONG 1000000\nSET LONGCHUNKSIZE 250"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 1. The recording at a glance\n\nThe watch has been sending since Jonas started at 10:05.\n\n**Question:** How much data has arrived, and how late did some of it arrive?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The watch sends a measuring point every 5 seconds: position, elevation, heart rate, cadence (steps per minute) and the peak acceleration in g. In the dead zone it buffers its points and sends them later (`BUFFERED = TRUE`).\n\n**Result:** 2,051 points between 10:05:00 and 12:55:50, 135 of them buffered; the longest delay is 11 minutes 17 seconds."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT device_id,\n       patient_id,\n       COUNT(*)                              AS points,\n       TO_CHAR(MIN(event_ts), 'HH24:MI:SS')  AS first_point,\n       TO_CHAR(MAX(event_ts), 'HH24:MI:SS')  AS last_point,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN buffered THEN 1 END)  AS buffered_points,\n       MAX(ingest_ts - event_ts)             AS max_delay\nFROM   watch_stream\nGROUP  BY device_id, patient_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 2. Late data: event time versus arrival time\n\nOn the climb to the Hohe Leite there is no mobile signal. The watch keeps measuring, but its points arrive minutes later in a burst.\n\n**Question:** What does the dead zone do to an analysis by arrival time?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The same points per minute, counted once by event time and once by arrival time. Between about 10:25 and 10:36 nothing arrives, then all buffered points arrive at once.\n\nA query that groups by arrival time sees a gap and a burst. Grouped by event time, the walk is continuous. This is why the stream is evaluated by event time.\n\n**Result:** by event time 12 points every minute. By arrival time nothing from 10:25 to 10:35, then 146 points at 10:36 – the 135 buffered points plus those of that minute. An analysis by arrival time would see an 11-minute standstill that never happened."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH by_event AS (\n  SELECT TRUNC(CAST(event_ts AS DATE), 'MI')  AS minute,\n         COUNT(*)                             AS by_event_time\n  FROM   watch_stream\n  GROUP  BY TRUNC(CAST(event_ts AS DATE), 'MI')\n),\nby_arrival AS (\n  SELECT TRUNC(CAST(ingest_ts AS DATE), 'MI') AS minute,\n         COUNT(*)                             AS by_arrival_time\n  FROM   watch_stream\n  GROUP  BY TRUNC(CAST(ingest_ts AS DATE), 'MI')\n)\nSELECT TO_CHAR(minute, 'HH24:MI')    AS minute,\n       NVL(e.by_event_time, 0)       AS points_by_event_time,\n       NVL(a.by_arrival_time, 0)     AS points_by_arrival_time\nFROM   by_event e\nFULL   OUTER JOIN by_arrival a USING (minute)\nWHERE  minute BETWEEN TO_DATE('2026-11-16 10:20', 'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI')\n                  AND TO_DATE('2026-11-16 10:40', 'YYYY-MM-DD HH24:MI')\nORDER  BY minute;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 3. Where were the late points measured? (dead zone)\n\nLate points can only be trusted if we know why they are late.\n\n**Question:** Where were the delayed points measured?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The spatial operator `SDO_ANYINTERACT` finds all watch points inside the dead zone `Z01` (*Funkloch Anstieg Hohe Leite*). The spatial index on `WATCH_STREAM` does the work; the join condition is the operator.\n\n**Result:** all 135 buffered points lie in the dead zone, measured from 10:24:50 to 10:36:00, on average 342 seconds late; no point outside the zone was buffered. The location explains the delay, so the analysis can trust them."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT z.name                                    AS zone,\n       w.buffered,\n       COUNT(*)                                  AS points,\n       TO_CHAR(MIN(w.event_ts), 'HH24:MI:SS')    AS measured_from,\n       TO_CHAR(MAX(w.event_ts), 'HH24:MI:SS')    AS measured_to,\n       ROUND(AVG((CAST(w.ingest_ts AS DATE) - CAST(w.event_ts AS DATE)) * 86400)) AS avg_delay_s\nFROM   watch_stream w\nJOIN   zones z\n  ON   SDO_ANYINTERACT(w.geom, z.geom) = 'TRUE'\nWHERE  z.zone_id = 'Z01'\nGROUP  BY z.name, w.buffered;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 4. Speed and grade over the last minute\n\nJust before 11:03 Jonas was descending a steep section of the loop.\n\n**Question:** How fast and how steep was he moving in the minutes around the fall?\n\n**How the query answers it:** GPS positions jitter by a few metres, so the step from one point to the next (5 s) is too noisy. `LAG(…, 12) OVER (ORDER BY event_ts)` gives the point one minute earlier instead. `SDO_GEOM.SDO_DISTANCE` (a function, allowed in the select list) measures the distance in metres between both positions; with the elevation difference this gives speed and grade over the last minute.\n\nThe window shows the two minutes around the fall.\n\n**Result:** until 11:03:05 Jonas descends at about 4 km/h on a grade of −16 to −26 % – the segment *Abstieg Felsstufe* has −18.9 %. At 11:03:10 a single peak of 4.9 g, then no more steps. The speed over the last minute falls gradually to under 1 km/h, because the one-minute window still contains walking points; it is not Jonas walking on. During the standstill the grade is noise (a few metres, jumping elevation values)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH points AS (\n  SELECT w.event_ts, w.lon, w.lat, w.ele_m, w.cadence_spm, w.acc_peak_g,\n         LAG(w.event_ts, 12) OVER (ORDER BY w.event_ts) AS ts_1min,\n         LAG(w.lon,      12) OVER (ORDER BY w.event_ts) AS lon_1min,\n         LAG(w.lat,      12) OVER (ORDER BY w.event_ts) AS lat_1min,\n         LAG(w.ele_m,    12) OVER (ORDER BY w.event_ts) AS ele_1min\n  FROM   watch_stream w\n  WHERE  w.patient_id = 'P0001'\n),\nminutes AS (\n  SELECT p.*,\n         SDO_GEOM.SDO_DISTANCE(\n           SDO_GEOMETRY(2001, 4326, SDO_POINT_TYPE(p.lon_1min, p.lat_1min, NULL), NULL, NULL),\n           SDO_GEOMETRY(2001, 4326, SDO_POINT_TYPE(p.lon,      p.lat,      NULL), NULL, NULL),\n           0.05, 'unit=M')                                          AS dist_m,\n         (CAST(p.event_ts AS DATE) - CAST(p.ts_1min AS DATE)) * 86400 AS dt_s\n  FROM   points p\n  WHERE  p.ts_1min IS NOT NULL\n)\nSELECT TO_CHAR(event_ts, 'HH24:MI:SS')                        AS time,\n       cadence_spm,\n       acc_peak_g,\n       ROUND(dist_m / NULLIF(dt_s, 0) * 3.6, 1)               AS speed_last_min_kmh,\n       ROUND((ele_m - ele_1min) / NULLIF(dist_m, 0) * 100)    AS grade_last_min_pct\nFROM   minutes\nWHERE  event_ts BETWEEN TIMESTAMP '2026-11-16 11:02:00 Europe/Berlin'\n                    AND TIMESTAMP '2026-11-16 11:04:00 Europe/Berlin'\nORDER  BY event_ts;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 5. Detect the event pattern: walking → impact → standstill → limping\n\nA fall looks the same in every stream: walking, a hard impact, standstill, then a different gait.\n\n**Question:** Does the stream contain this pattern – and when exactly did Jonas fall?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`MATCH_RECOGNIZE` finds a sequence of rows like a regular expression, ordered by event time:\n\n| Variable | Condition | Meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| `walk{12,}` | cadence ≥ 90 steps/min, at least 1 minute | normal hiking |\n| `impact` | acceleration peak ≥ 3 g | the fall |\n| `still{6,}` | cadence 0, no peaks, at least 30 s | standstill |\n| `limp{6,}` | cadence 30 to 80 steps/min | limping |\n\nThe column `PHASE` in the table is the ground truth of the synthetic data; the pattern does not use it.\n\n**Result:** one match: walking from 10:05:00 without interruption – the buffered points of the dead zone included, because the stream is ordered by event time –, the fall at 11:03:10 with 4.9 g, 8 minutes of standstill until 11:11:10, then limping from 11:11:15 with about 58 steps per minute."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT patient_id,\n       TO_CHAR(walk_start,     'HH24:MI:SS') AS walk_start,\n       TO_CHAR(fall_ts,        'HH24:MI:SS') AS fall_time,\n       impact_g,\n       TO_CHAR(standstill_end, 'HH24:MI:SS') AS standstill_end,\n       standstill_min,\n       TO_CHAR(limping_start,  'HH24:MI:SS') AS limping_start,\n       limping_cadence\nFROM   watch_stream\nMATCH_RECOGNIZE (\n  PARTITION BY patient_id\n  ORDER BY event_ts\n  MEASURES FIRST(walk.event_ts)        AS walk_start,\n           impact.event_ts             AS fall_ts,\n           impact.acc_peak_g           AS impact_g,\n           LAST(still.event_ts)        AS standstill_end,\n           ROUND((CAST(LAST(still.event_ts) AS DATE) - CAST(impact.event_ts AS DATE)) * 1440, 1)\n                                       AS standstill_min,\n           FIRST(limp.event_ts)        AS limping_start,\n           ROUND(AVG(limp.cadence_spm)) AS limping_cadence\n  ONE ROW PER MATCH\n  AFTER MATCH SKIP PAST LAST ROW\n  PATTERN (walk{12,} impact still{6,} limp{6,})\n  DEFINE walk   AS walk.cadence_spm >= 90,\n           impact AS impact.acc_peak_g >= 3,\n           still  AS still.cadence_spm = 0 AND still.acc_peak_g < 2,\n           limp   AS limp.cadence_spm BETWEEN 30 AND 80\n);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 6. Where did it happen? Map the fall to the trail segment\n\nA time is not enough: the friend, the rescue service and the knowledge graph need the place.\n\n**Question:** On which trail segment did Jonas fall?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The position of the `impact` row becomes a point geometry. `SDO_NN` (nearest neighbour) finds the closest trail segment; `SDO_NN_DISTANCE(1)` returns the distance for the operator with the same number. The fall is a single row, so its point goes into `SDO_NN` as a scalar subquery: the spatial index of `TRAIL_SEGMENTS` searches around one fixed point, and no join is needed. `DEFINE` only describes the variables that the `PATTERN` uses (here without `limp`).\n\n**Result:** the fall happened on the segment *Abstieg Felsstufe* (T1-S08), less than a metre from the trail line – the steepest part of the loop with a grade of −18.9 %."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH fall AS (\n  SELECT patient_id, fall_ts, fall_lon, fall_lat\n  FROM   watch_stream\n  MATCH_RECOGNIZE (\n    PARTITION BY patient_id\n    ORDER BY event_ts\n    MEASURES impact.event_ts AS fall_ts,\n             impact.lon      AS fall_lon,\n             impact.lat      AS fall_lat\n    ONE ROW PER MATCH\n    PATTERN (walk{12,} impact still{6,})\n    DEFINE walk   AS walk.cadence_spm >= 90,\n           impact AS impact.acc_peak_g >= 3,\n           still  AS still.cadence_spm = 0 AND still.acc_peak_g < 2\n  )\n)\nSELECT (SELECT f.patient_id FROM fall f)                     AS patient_id,\n       (SELECT TO_CHAR(f.fall_ts, 'HH24:MI:SS') FROM fall f) AS fall_time,\n       t.segment_id,\n       t.segment_name,\n       t.trail_name,\n       t.grade_pct,\n       t.difficulty,\n       ROUND(SDO_NN_DISTANCE(1), 1)                          AS distance_m\nFROM   trail_segments t\nWHERE  SDO_NN(t.geom,\n              (SELECT SDO_GEOMETRY(2001, 4326, SDO_POINT_TYPE(f.fall_lon, f.fall_lat, NULL), NULL, NULL)\n               FROM   fall f),\n              'sdo_num_res=1 unit=M', 1) = 'TRUE';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 7. The steepest segments of the loop\n\nBad luck or a dangerous spot?\n\n**Question:** Is the segment of the fall the steepest part of the loop?\n\n**How the query answers it:** For comparison: the segments of the same trail ordered by the absolute grade.\n\n**Result:** yes. *Abstieg Felsstufe* (T1-S08) drops 35 m over 185 m (−18.9 %, *schwer*) and is clearly the steepest segment, ahead of *Abstieg Steilstück* (−12.1 %, *schwer*) and *Hangweg* (−10.5 %, *mittel*). Jonas fell exactly where the trail is most demanding – a terrain detail the diagnosis can use later."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT segment_id,\n       segment_name,\n       length_m,\n       elev_from_m,\n       elev_to_m,\n       grade_pct,\n       difficulty\nFROM   trail_segments\nWHERE  trail_id = (SELECT MIN(trail_id) FROM trail_segments WHERE segment_name = 'Abstieg Felsstufe')\nORDER  BY ABS(grade_pct) DESC\nFETCH  FIRST 3 ROWS ONLY;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 8. After the fall: how fast does Jonas still move?\n\nAfter a few minutes Jonas moves again – but how?\n\n**Question:** Can Jonas walk back on his own?\n\n**How the query answers it:** With `ALL ROWS PER MATCH`, `MATCH_RECOGNIZE` returns every row of the match, and `CLASSIFIER()` names the pattern variable each row belongs to. For every point the speed over the last minute is computed as in section 4; the median per detected phase is robust against GPS jitter.\n\n**Result:** a median of 3.9 km/h while hiking (from 10:06:00 – the first minute has no earlier point to compare with), 0.3 km/h in the standstill – GPS jitter, not movement – and only 1.3 km/h while limping from 11:11:15 to 11:21:30. The impact row still shows 3.5 km/h, the walking speed of the minute before. Jonas can no longer walk back on his own – the event that triggers the next steps (pattern M4)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH classified AS (\n  SELECT patient_id, event_ts, lon, lat, detected_phase\n  FROM   watch_stream\n  MATCH_RECOGNIZE (\n    PARTITION BY patient_id\n    ORDER BY event_ts\n    MEASURES CLASSIFIER() AS detected_phase\n    ALL ROWS PER MATCH\n    AFTER MATCH SKIP PAST LAST ROW\n    PATTERN (walk{12,} impact still{6,} limp{6,})\n    DEFINE walk   AS walk.cadence_spm >= 90,\n           impact AS impact.acc_peak_g >= 3,\n           still  AS still.cadence_spm = 0 AND still.acc_peak_g < 2,\n           limp   AS limp.cadence_spm BETWEEN 30 AND 80\n  )\n),\npoints AS (\n  SELECT c.*,\n         LAG(c.event_ts, 12) OVER (ORDER BY c.event_ts) AS ts_1min,\n         LAG(c.lon,      12) OVER (ORDER BY c.event_ts) AS lon_1min,\n         LAG(c.lat,      12) OVER (ORDER BY c.event_ts) AS lat_1min\n  FROM   classified c\n),\nspeeds AS (\n  SELECT p.detected_phase,\n         p.event_ts,\n         SDO_GEOM.SDO_DISTANCE(\n           SDO_GEOMETRY(2001, 4326, SDO_POINT_TYPE(p.lon_1min, p.lat_1min, NULL), NULL, NULL),\n           SDO_GEOMETRY(2001, 4326, SDO_POINT_TYPE(p.lon,      p.lat,      NULL), NULL, NULL),\n           0.05, 'unit=M')\n         / ((CAST(p.event_ts AS DATE) - CAST(p.ts_1min AS DATE)) * 86400) * 3.6 AS speed_kmh\n  FROM   points p\n  WHERE  p.ts_1min IS NOT NULL\n)\nSELECT detected_phase,\n       COUNT(*)                              AS points,\n       TO_CHAR(MIN(event_ts), 'HH24:MI:SS')  AS from_time,\n       TO_CHAR(MAX(event_ts), 'HH24:MI:SS')  AS to_time,\n       ROUND(MEDIAN(speed_kmh), 1)           AS median_speed_kmh\nFROM   speeds\nGROUP  BY detected_phase\nORDER  BY MIN(event_ts);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Summary\n\n- The stream is evaluated by event time; late points from the dead zone are sorted in correctly.\n- `MATCH_RECOGNIZE` detects walking → impact → standstill → limping; spatial operators place the fall on the segment *Abstieg Felsstufe*.\n- Since 11:03 Jonas moves at only about 1 to 1.5 km/h. The event triggers the search for similar cases.\n- Next: **11c** – what do similar cases say?"
    languageId: markdown
